robin  <usenet@unrated.net> wrote:
>In message <10qsu50j4cam5f2@corp.supernews.com>, Ben Scott wrote:
>>How can this death happen?
>>turned to slime by a cockatrice egg
>I noticed that as I was doing the uniqueness categories; I'd also _very_ much
>like to know that.

It's a bug; all timeout deaths set delayed_killer, so if delayed-action
death B is started after death A it resets delayed_killer, but then if A
takes effect first (or on the same turn, but is first in u.uprops) you get
a method message from A as timeout.c invokes done() - here 
done (TURNED_SLIME) but delayed_killer is still appropriate to B.

One could also be "petrified by a green slime", I think. If you've got
both, it looks like deliberate exploitation. Deathly sickness tracks its
cause separately, but self-genocide while polyed might also interact oddly
with this; I don't have the time to check.

It seems to me, also, that sliming should trump stoning. You can't be
turning into both, and slimes are acidic.
